\description{
For each segment, the fraction of its bins whose copy-number state matches
the state a reference segmentation assigns to the same bins.

This is the state-aware, size-neutral per-segment score. A short segment
whose bins are all correctly stated scores 1; a segment that overlaps its
counterpart perfectly but calls the wrong copy number scores near 0. It is
the complement of \code{\link[=annotateBinJaccard]{annotateBinJaccard()}}, which rewards positional overlap
and is indifferent to state.

The counters, not just the fraction, are attached, because they are what
aggregates correctly: pooling \code{state.n_matched} over \code{state.n_total} across
segments gives the exon-weighted accuracy of the whole segmentation, whereas
averaging the per-segment fractions weights a two-bin segment as heavily as a
two-thousand-bin one. Both are useful and they are not the same number; see
\code{\link[=summariseStateConcordance]{summariseStateConcordance()}}, which reports each.
}
\section{Direction}{

The function is directional and both directions are worth computing.
Annotating the prediction against the truth gives something that behaves like
precision -- of what this segment claims, how much is right. Annotating the
truth against the prediction gives recall -- of what was really there, how
much was found. The pipeline that produced this project's
\verb{*_segments_jaccard_predicted.tsv} and \verb{*_segments_jaccard_truth.tsv} files
runs both.
}

\section{NA handling}{

A segment with no bin carrying a defined reference state gets
\code{state.concordance = NA} and zero counters -- there was nothing to score it
against. That is distinct from a concordance of 0, which means the segment
had bins and got all of them wrong, and the two must not be conflated when
aggregating: see \code{\link[=summariseStateConcordance]{summariseStateConcordance()}}.
}

\section{Choosing the bins}{

\code{bins} is required and has no default on purpose. On whole-exome data a
uniform base-pair tiling is not a discretisation but a fabrication: most of
each tile is unsequenced, no caller had evidence there, and the metric
degenerates into a base-pair agreement measured largely over intergenic
space. Pass the capture target regions. On whole-genome data a
\code{\link[GenomicRanges:tileGenome]{GenomicRanges::tileGenome()}} grid is appropriate.

The bins also act as the region filter. To exclude the sex chromosomes --
which otherwise dominate the score on samples with no autosomal events --
pass autosome-only bins. There is deliberately no separate \code{drop_seqnames}
argument: one mechanism, not two.
